This is a change to the amdgpu driver to add some Barco adapter support.

You need to build a separate kernel package, per this URL:

https://kernel-team.pages.debian.net/kernel-handbook/ch-common-tasks.html#s-common-official

and use it to upgrade the CODE_DARK bootable image we create.

This cannot be easily automated.

The amdgpu.c driver file gets the patch applied from the tree.

Here is, in short, the steps needed to be taken under Debian 13.

1. Update to the latest (sudo apt update; sudo apt upgrade -y). Reboot
if the kernel was updated.

2. Find a build directory- next to where you have this tree checked out is good.
Go there.

3. Issue these commands:

    sudo apt-get install build-essential
    sudo apt-get build-dep linux
    apt-get source linux
    cd linux-*
    export MAKEFLAGS=-j$(nproc)
    export DEB_BUILD_PROFILES='pkg.linux.nokerneldbg pkg.linux.nokerneldbginfo'

4. Modify changelog.

Edit debian/changelog and stick this clause at the top (enclosed between ++++):

++++
linux (6.12.2030-1~exp1) experimental; urgency=medium

* Patches to amdgpu driver for Barco adapters:
[ Matthew Jacob ]
* Manually apply update to recognize 2 nominally windows only Barco adapters

-- Matthew Jacob <lydianconcepts@gmail.com>  Mon, 11 May 2026 12:54:02 +0800
++++

Change the name/email address for you. Probably to make sure you are higher
than other packages, change 2030-1 to 2031-1 or something.

5. Patch the amd gpu driver (assuming we are a peer tree to cdpw):

 patch -p1 < ../../cdpw/kernel-changes/amdgpu_drv.c.diff

If this fails, you'll have to figure out how to modify things.

6. Build

 dpkg-buildpackage -B -nc -uc

This may fail once at first (telling you why it is, but you can ignore that).
The last failure is known but you should still have

  linux-image-6.12.XX+deb13-amd64-unsigned_6.12.XX-1_amd64.deb

In the directory one above here. This is what you'll be installing on
the USB key. The XX will change depending upon when you fork the
source to rebuild the kernel.







  git commit -am'add support for additional Barco adapters'

(obviously look for failure to patch correctly!).

Edit the changelog under debian.<version>, e.g. debian.hwe-6.14/changelog
so as to make a distinct ABI to make this different from the standard
release. Here is an example diff:

index 1ff980a1987c..a964fd32bbac 100644
--- a/debian.hwe-6.14/changelog
+++ b/debian.hwe-6.14/changelog
@@ -1,4 +1,4 @@
-linux-hwe-6.14 (6.14.0-32.32~24.04.1) noble; urgency=medium
+linux-hwe-6.14 (6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1) noble; urgency=medium

* noble/linux-hwe-6.14: 6.14.0-32.32~24.04.1 -proposed tracker (LP: #2120124)

Now you can just build more or less as documented, but here's
a cheat sheet:

  chmod a+x debian/rules
  chmod a+x debian/scripts/*
  chmod a+x debian/scripts/misc/*
  fakeroot debian/rules clean
  fakeroot debian/rules binary

Go out for lunch and to the gym- even on a fast system with an
SSD, it'll take a few hours.

When it completes, the directory above will have all of the requisite
packages. In the example case:

  mjacob@orthanc > ls -1 ../*.deb
  ../linux-buildinfo-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-cloud-tools-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-headers-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-hwe-6.14-cloud-tools-6.14.0-1030_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-hwe-6.14-headers-6.14.0-1030_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_all.deb
  ../linux-hwe-6.14-tools-6.14.0-1030_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-image-unsigned-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-lib-rust-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-modules-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-modules-extra-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-modules-ipu6-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-modules-ipu7-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-modules-iwlwifi-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-modules-usbio-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-modules-vision-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-tools-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb

You only really need 3:

  linux-image-unsigned-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  linux-modules-extra-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  linux-modules-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb

Put this into a tarball and when you are creating a gold master, you'll do
the following:

Clean /var/tmp:
  sudo rm -f /var/tmp/linux*.deb

Extra from your tarball those packages to /var/tmp and do:

  sudo apt install /var/tmp/linux*.deb

This should install and the next boot should give you a kernel with extra
Barco support which is the only reason this is being done at all.
